      \field{abstract}{Foxa transcription factors help endow the competence to activate liver genes in development by binding to regulatory sequences in precursor endoderm and opening a local domain of chromatin. Fibroblast growth factors and bone morphogenetic proteins, which are secreted from different mesodermal cell types, induce the hepatic gene programme in the endoderm, whereas they inhibit the pancreatic programme. Transcription factors Hex and Prox1 are crucial for nascent hepatoblasts to undergo a transition from an endodermal epithelium to a non-polarized, migrating cell that proliferates more rapidly, thereby creating a liver bud. Expansion of a liver bud into a haematopoietic organ, during fetal development, requires a diverse array of growth signals, including those from the septum transversum mesenchyme and from primitive endothelial cells that surround the liver bud, before blood-vessel formation. Distinct transcriptional cascades have been identified that promote the differentiation of hepatocytes and bile-duct cells from early hepatoblasts. The transcription factors Hnf4 (hepatocyte nuclear factor-4) and C/EBPα (CCAAT/enhancer binding protein-α) promote the terminal differentiation of hepatocytes as the organ ceases to be a site of haematopoiesis and begins to control metabolite and protein levels in the blood, during the perinatal period.}
